DESK.chapters.push(
{id:'e1',n:1,code:'HB 1',ref:'Hornbill, Chapter 1',libLabel:'1. The Portrait of a Lady',name:'The Portrait of a Lady',unit:'prose',pdf:'kehb101',
lesson:{intro:'Khushwant Singh’s memoir of his grandmother traces how their close childhood friendship slowly changes as he grows up, and ends with her peaceful death, mourned silently by the sparrows she fed.',
ideas:['Village years: she wakes him, gets him ready, walks him to the school attached to the temple, and feeds the village dogs with stale chapattis on the way back.','City years: an English school, a motor bus and lessons she cannot help with. She is upset that there is no teaching about God and the scriptures, and that he learns music, which she links with harlots and beggars. This is the turning point of their friendship.','University years: he gets a room of his own and they meet less. She accepts her loneliness and spends afternoons feeding sparrows that perch on her legs, shoulders and head.','He goes abroad for five years. At the station she is calm, praying and telling her beads, and kisses his forehead.','On his return she celebrates all evening, singing homecoming songs on an old drum with the women of the neighbourhood. The next morning she falls ill, says her end is near, and spends her last hours praying and telling her beads.','Thousands of sparrows sit silently around her body and ignore the crumbs; they fly away only when her body is carried off.'],
formulas:['Author: Khushwant Singh · Genre: memoir / character sketch · Narrator: first person','Image: “like the winter landscape in the mountains” — pure white serenity, peace and contentment','Traits: deeply religious, loving, dignified, self-reliant, accepting of change','Themes: changing relationships with age, generation gap, faith, death with dignity','Symbols: rosary (faith), sparrows (her bond with nature; silent mourning)'],
example:'<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>How did the sparrows react to the grandmother’s death?</em> Thousands of sparrows sat silently around her body in the verandah and her room. They did not chirp and ignored the bread crumbs the author’s mother threw. They flew away quietly only when the body was carried off, as if mourning a friend.</p>',
trap:'The turning point in the friendship is the move to the city and the English school, not the author’s going abroad.',
shortcut:'Remember the story as three stages, village → city → university, then the farewell, the homecoming and her death. Every short-answer question falls into one of these.',
exam:'Turning points in the relationship, the grandmother’s character and religious faith, the homecoming celebration, and the sparrows’ silent tribute.'},
q:[
['E','Who wrote “The Portrait of a Lady”?',['Khushwant Singh','Ruskin Bond','R.K. Narayan','Mulk Raj Anand'],'It is Khushwant Singh’s memoir of his grandmother.','N'],
['E','How does the author remember his grandmother?',['As always old and wrinkled','As young and beautiful','As a strict schoolteacher','As someone he never met'],'In the twenty years he knew her, she had always looked old and wrinkled.','N'],
['E','In his portrait, the grandfather wore',['a big turban and loose-fitting clothes','a suit and tie','a soldier’s uniform','a priest’s robe'],'He looked too old to have had a wife and children.','N'],
['E','In the village, where did the grandmother take the author to study?',['A school attached to the temple','An English school in the city','A mission school','A boarding school'],'The priest taught the alphabet and the morning prayer there.','N'],
['E','In the village, the grandmother fed the dogs with',['stale chapattis','rice and milk','biscuits','meat scraps'],'She carried stale chapattis on the way back from school.','N'],
['E','The grandmother’s lips constantly moved in',['inaudible prayer','song','complaint','conversation with neighbours'],'She was always praying, usually while telling the beads of her rosary.','N'],
['E','The grandmother always dressed in',['spotless white','bright colours','black','red and gold'],'Spotless white, which adds to her image of purity and serenity.','N'],
['E','In the city, how did the author travel to school?',['By motor bus','On foot with his grandmother','By bicycle','By horse cart'],'This is one reason she could no longer accompany him.','N'],
['E','In the city, the grandmother spent her afternoons feeding',['sparrows','dogs','pigeons','cows'],'Sparrows perched on her legs, shoulders and head.','N'],
['E','For how long did the author go abroad?',['Five years','Two years','Ten years','One year'],'He went abroad for further studies for five years.','N'],
['M','What was the turning point in the friendship between the author and his grandmother?',['Moving to the city and joining an English school','His going abroad','Her illness','His getting married'],'In the city she could no longer help him with lessons, and they shared less and less.','N'],
['M','Why was the grandmother upset when the author started music lessons?',['She believed music was for harlots and beggars, not for gentlefolk','She thought it was too expensive','She wanted him to learn dance instead','She disliked the music teacher'],'She associated music with low company and disapproved silently.','N'],
['M','The comparison of the grandmother to “the winter landscape in the mountains” suggests her',['pure white serenity and peace','coldness and harshness','loneliness and sorrow','great height'],'The image conveys calm, purity and contentment.','N'],
['M','How did the grandmother behave at the railway station when the author left for abroad?',['She was calm, praying and telling her beads, and kissed his forehead','She cried loudly','She refused to come','She begged him not to go'],'She showed no outward emotion, only quiet prayer.','N'],
['M','How did the grandmother celebrate the author’s return from abroad?',['She sang homecoming songs on an old drum with the neighbourhood women','She threw a large dinner party','She went to the temple for a week','She gave sweets to the sparrows'],'She sang about the homecoming of warriors for hours, overstraining herself.','N'],
['M','Why did the grandmother dislike the English school?',['It taught nothing about God and the scriptures','It was far away','The teachers were unkind','It had no playground'],'She was distressed that science, not faith, was taught there.','N'],
['M','What did the grandmother do when the author got a room of his own?',['She accepted her seclusion quietly and spent more time with her spinning wheel and sparrows','She protested strongly','She moved back to the village at once','She stopped speaking to everyone'],'She accepted the change with resignation.','N'],
['M','The detail that she hobbled about “with one hand resting on her waist to balance her stoop” shows her',['old age and physical frailty','anger','wealth','youthfulness'],'It is a vivid picture of an old, bent woman.','N'],
['M','Where did the sparrows perch while she fed them?',['On her legs, shoulders and even her head','Only on the window sill','On the roof','On the garden wall'],'They trusted her completely.','N'],
['M','Assertion (A): The grandmother was distressed when the author began music lessons.<br>Reason (R): She considered music fit only for harlots and beggars.','AR1','Both are true, and R is the reason for A.','B'],
['H','What did the grandmother do after she fell ill and sensed her end was near?',['She stopped talking, lay in bed praying and telling her beads','She called all her relatives','She asked for a doctor from abroad','She went back to the village'],'She did not want to waste her last hours talking.','N'],
['H','How did the sparrows behave after the grandmother’s death?',['They sat silently around her body and ignored the crumbs','They chirped loudly','They ate all the crumbs','They flew away at once'],'Their silence was a tribute; they left only when her body was carried away.','N'],
['H','What happened to the bread crumbs the next morning?',['The sweeper swept them into the dustbin','The sparrows ate them','The author kept them','They were thrown to the dogs'],'This shows the sparrows had truly mourned rather than fed.','N'],
['H','Which theme is central to the chapter?',['The changing relationship between generations','Rural poverty','Political freedom','Life in a boarding school'],'It follows how a close bond changes as the author grows up.','N'],
['H','After her death, her face is described as having',['a peaceful pallor','a frightened look','a bright smile','deep wrinkles of pain'],'It suggests she died at peace.','N'],
['H','Which set of traits best describes the grandmother?',['Deeply religious, loving and dignified','Strict, bitter and selfish','Modern and fashionable','Talkative and restless'],'Her prayers, care for the author and calm acceptance show these traits.','N'],
['H','The story is narrated in the',['first person, by the grandson','third person, by an outsider','second person','first person, by the grandmother'],'The author tells it from his own memories.','N'],
['H','Which detail best shows the growing distance between them in the city?',['She could not help him with lessons and did not believe what the school taught','She refused to cook for him','She moved to another house','She stopped praying'],'English, science and music were outside her world.','N'],
['H','Assertion (A): The grandmother showed no emotion when the author left for abroad.<br>Reason (R): By then she no longer loved him.','AR3','A is true. R is false: she loved him deeply; her calm came from faith and dignity, as her joyful celebration on his return shows.','B'],
['H','“The Portrait of a Lady” is best described as',['a memoir and character sketch','a science fiction story','a one-act play','a travelogue'],'It paints a portrait of a real person from the author’s life.','N']
]},
{id:'e2',n:2,code:'HB 2',ref:'Hornbill, Chapter 2',libLabel:'2. We’re Not Afraid to Die…',name:'We’re Not Afraid to Die… if We Can All Be Together',unit:'prose',pdf:'kehb102',
lesson:{intro:'A true account of a family’s round-the-world voyage that turns into a fight for survival in the southern Indian Ocean. It is a story of courage, calm thinking and the strength a family draws from being together.',
ideas:['In July 1976 the narrator, his wife Mary, son Jonathan (6) and daughter Suzanne (7) set sail from Plymouth to repeat Captain James Cook’s round-the-world voyage.','Their boat, the Wavewalker, was a 23-metre, 30-ton wooden-hulled ship, professionally built and tested in rough weather.','From Cape Town they took on two crewmen, the American Larry Vigil and the Swiss Herb Seigler, to cross the rough southern Indian Ocean.','On 2 January a huge wave smashed the deck. The narrator was injured (cracked ribs, broken teeth), water poured in, and they pumped for hours. A spare electric pump was connected to keep them afloat.','Sue hid a serious head injury so as not to worry her parents; Jon said, “We are not afraid of dying if we can all be together.”','With careful navigation the narrator found tiny Île Amsterdam, a French scientific base, and the family was saved. Jon called him “the best daddy … and the best captain”.'],
formulas:['Authors: Gordon Cook and Alan East · Genre: true adventure narrative','Boat: Wavewalker · Start: Plymouth, July 1976 · Crisis: southern Indian Ocean, 2 January','Crew: Larry Vigil (American), Herb Seigler (Swiss) · Rescue: Île Amsterdam','Themes: courage in crisis, optimism, presence of mind, family solidarity','Characters: calm, skilled narrator; brave, selfless children'],
example:'<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>How did the children show courage?</em> Jonathan stayed calm and told his father they were not afraid to die if they were together. Suzanne hid a serious head injury so as not to add to her parents’ worries, and even made a funny card to cheer them up.</p>',
trap:'Don’t mix up the crew members’ nationalities: Larry Vigil was American, Herb Seigler was Swiss.',
shortcut:'Build answers on three qualities: the narrator’s presence of mind, the family’s togetherness, and the children’s maturity.',
exam:'The role of the narrator in the crisis, the courage of the children, the meaning of the title, and how optimism helps in danger.'},
q:[
['E','Who wrote “We’re Not Afraid to Die… if We Can All Be Together”?',['Gordon Cook and Alan East','Jayant Narlikar','Nick Middleton','A.R. Williams'],'It is their account of a real voyage.','N'],
['E','From which port did the family begin their voyage?',['Plymouth, England','Cape Town','Sydney','Mumbai'],'They set sail from Plymouth in July 1976.','N'],
['E','In which year did the voyage begin?',['1976','1950','1990','2001'],'July 1976.','N'],
['E','What was the name of the boat?',['Wavewalker','Sea Queen','Ocean Star','Endeavour'],'A 23-metre, 30-ton wooden-hulled boat.','N'],
['E','The family wanted to repeat the round-the-world voyage made 200 years earlier by',['Captain James Cook','Christopher Columbus','Vasco da Gama','Ferdinand Magellan'],'That was the aim of the trip.','N'],
['E','The narrator’s children were',['Jonathan and Suzanne','Larry and Herb','Tom and Mary','Andrew and Christine'],'Jonathan was six and Suzanne seven.','N'],
['E','The two crewmen hired at Cape Town were',['Larry Vigil, an American, and Herb Seigler, a Swiss','two British sailors','two Australian fishermen','the narrator’s brothers'],'They joined to help in the roughest seas.','N'],
['E','In which ocean did the disaster strike?',['The southern Indian Ocean','The Atlantic Ocean','The Arctic Ocean','The Pacific Ocean near Hawaii'],'Between Cape Town and Australia.','N'],
['E','How old was Suzanne at the time of the voyage?',['Seven','Six','Ten','Twelve'],'Jonathan was six.','N'],
['E','Which island did the family finally reach?',['Île Amsterdam','Sri Lanka','Madagascar','Tasmania'],'A tiny French scientific base in the Indian Ocean.','N'],
['M','Where did the family celebrate Christmas?',['On board the boat, in the ocean east of Cape Town','In a hotel in Cape Town','On Île Amsterdam','At home in England'],'They held a Christmas celebration on board.','N'],
['M','On which date did the huge wave strike the boat?',['2 January','25 December','15 July','1 April'],'The crisis began on 2 January.','N'],
['M','What injuries did the narrator suffer when the wave struck?',['Cracked ribs and broken teeth','A broken leg','Burns from the engine','No injuries at all'],'His mouth filled with blood from a broken tooth.','N'],
['M','What injury did Sue have?',['A head injury, with a large bump and swollen eyes','A broken arm','A cut on her foot','None'],'She hid how badly she was hurt.','N'],
['M','How did the narrator deal with the failure of the electric pump?',['He found a spare pump and connected it to an outlet pipe','He gave up and waited','He swam to the shore','He called a passing ship'],'His practical thinking kept the boat afloat.','N'],
['M','What does Jon’s statement “We are not afraid of dying if we can all be together” reveal?',['Courage and the strength of family togetherness','Fear of the sea','Anger at his parents','Boredom with the voyage'],'It shows a child’s courage and faith in his family.','N'],
['M','What did Sue’s card show?',['Funny caricatures of her parents, made to make them laugh','A map of the island','A list of supplies','A letter asking to go home'],'Even injured, she tried to cheer her parents up.','N'],
['M','Who kept pumping water out of the boat during the crisis?',['Larry and Herb','Only Jon','Nobody','Sailors from another ship'],'The crewmen pumped for long hours.','N'],
['M','Île Amsterdam is',['a small island with a French scientific base','a large city','a lighthouse in England','a port in Australia'],'Finding it in the vast ocean needed precise navigation.','N'],
['M','Assertion (A): The narrator managed to reach Île Amsterdam despite the damaged boat.<br>Reason (R): A passing ship rescued the family and towed the Wavewalker to the island.','AR3','A is true. R is false: no ship rescued them; the narrator navigated the damaged boat there himself.','B'],
['H','Describe the Wavewalker.',['A 23-metre, 30-ton wooden-hulled boat, professionally built','A small rubber dinghy','A steel cargo ship','A rented yacht with no testing'],'It had been tested in rough weather before the voyage.','N'],
['H','Why did the narrator hire two crewmen at Cape Town?',['To help them face one of the roughest seas in the world','Because Mary wanted to go home','To cook for the family','Because the boat was too big to steer'],'The southern Indian Ocean is known for its storms.','N'],
['H','When did Jon call his father “the best daddy in the whole world – and the best captain”?',['When they sighted Île Amsterdam','When the wave struck','At the Christmas party','In Plymouth before leaving'],'It was his praise for the father who saved them.','N'],
['H','Which theme is central to the chapter?',['Courage, optimism and presence of mind in a crisis','Greed for treasure','The joys of city life','Conflict between nations'],'The family survives by staying calm and together.','N'],
['H','Why did Sue not complain about her injury?',['She did not want to add to her parents’ worries','She did not feel any pain','She was asleep throughout','She was angry with her parents'],'Her silence shows unusual maturity.','N'],
['H','Who said, “We are not afraid of dying if we can all be together”?',['Jonathan','Suzanne','Mary','Larry'],'Jon said it to his father.','N'],
['H','Which word best describes the narrator during the crisis?',['Calm and resourceful','Panicky','Careless','Hopeless'],'He thought clearly and acted practically.','N'],
['H','What does the children’s behaviour during the storm show?',['Maturity and courage beyond their age','Fear and panic','Indifference','Disobedience'],'Both children supported their parents.','N'],
['H','Assertion (A): The family’s experience shows that their preparations for the voyage were useless.<br>Reason (R): Storms at sea cannot always be predicted.','AR4','A is false: the well-built boat and the narrator’s skill helped them survive. R is true.','B'],
['H','Assertion (A): The narrator stayed calm throughout the crisis.<br>Reason (R): Sue had a serious head injury.','AR2','Both are true, but Sue’s injury does not explain his calm; his presence of mind came from his experience and responsibility.','B']
]},
{id:'e3',n:3,code:'HB 3',ref:'Hornbill, Chapter 3',libLabel:'3. Discovering Tut',name:'Discovering Tut: the Saga Continues',unit:'prose',pdf:'kehb103',
lesson:{intro:'A factual report on how modern science, through a CT scan in 2005, set out to solve the mysteries of Tutankhamun, the boy king who died at nineteen, and how the discovery of his tomb in 1922 changed archaeology.',
ideas:['Tutankhamun was the last heir of a powerful family that ruled Egypt for centuries. He died at about 19.','Howard Carter discovered his tomb in the Valley of the Kings in 1922. It was full of treasures, including a solid gold coffin.','Ritual resins had hardened and cemented the mummy to the coffin. Carter’s team cut the body apart with chisels and heated knives to remove it.','In January 2005, a team led by Zahi Hawass (Egypt’s Supreme Council of Antiquities) CT-scanned the mummy in a portable scanner near the tomb, producing about 1,700 images.','Tut’s family history: Amenhotep III (possibly his grandfather); Akhenaten, who promoted worship of the Aten (the sun disk) and moved the capital to Amarna; the mysterious Smenkhkare; then Tutankhaten, who became Tutankhamun and restored the old gods.','The scan helped estimate his age and look for clues to his death; the mystery is not fully solved, so the “saga continues”.'],
formulas:['Author: A.R. Williams · Genre: informative, scientific report','1922: tomb found by Howard Carter · 2005: CT scan led by Zahi Hawass','CT = computed tomography: hundreds of X-ray images put together into a 3-D picture','Key names: Amenhotep III, Akhenaten (Aten worship, Amarna), Smenkhkare, Tutankhamun','Themes: science helping history, respect for the past, mystery of death'],
example:'<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Why did Carter’s team cut the mummy?</em> The ritual resins poured over the mummy had hardened like cement, fixing it to the bottom of the gold coffin. Since no legitimate force could free it, the team used chisels and heated knives and cut the body apart.</p>',
trap:'Carter discovered the tomb in 1922, but the CT scan was in 2005. Do not mix up the two dates or the two people.',
shortcut:'Make a two-column timeline: “then” (Tut’s family, his death, Carter in 1922) and “now” (Hawass and the CT scan in 2005).',
exam:'Why the mummy was damaged, why CT scanning was done, Akhenaten’s reforms, Tut’s family, and the role of science in history.'},
q:[
['E','Who wrote “Discovering Tut: the Saga Continues”?',['A.R. Williams','Nani Palkhivala','Howard Carter','Zahi Hawass'],'A.R. Williams reported on the scan.','N'],
['E','At what age did Tutankhamun die?',['About 19','About 50','About 9','About 70'],'He was a teenage king.','N'],
['E','Who discovered Tutankhamun’s tomb?',['Howard Carter','Zahi Hawass','A.R. Williams','Napoleon'],'The British archaeologist Howard Carter.','N'],
['E','In which year was Tutankhamun’s tomb discovered?',['1922','2005','1876','1947'],'The CT scan came much later, in 2005.','N'],
['E','Tutankhamun was the last heir of',['a powerful family that ruled Egypt for centuries','a family of merchants','a Roman dynasty','a Greek king'],'His death ended the family line.','N'],
['E','What modern technique was used to examine Tut’s mummy in 2005?',['A CT scan','Carbon dating only','A microscope','A metal detector'],'Computed tomography.','N'],
['E','In which year was Tut’s mummy CT-scanned?',['2005','1922','1960','2015'],'January 2005.','N'],
['E','Who led the team that scanned Tut’s mummy?',['Zahi Hawass','Howard Carter','Lord Carnarvon','Akhenaten'],'Head of Egypt’s Supreme Council of Antiquities.','N'],
['E','Tutankhamun’s tomb is in',['the Valley of the Kings','the Great Pyramid of Giza','Alexandria','Amarna'],'Near Luxor in Egypt.','N'],
['E','About how many images did the CT scan produce?',['1,700','17','170,000','70'],'About 1,700 digital X-ray images.','N'],
['M','Why did Carter’s team cut the mummy into pieces?',['Hardened ritual resins had cemented it to the coffin','They were searching for hidden gold inside','The body was already broken','They wanted to send parts to museums'],'No legitimate force could lift it out.','N'],
['M','What tools did Carter’s men use to free the mummy?',['Chisels and heated knives','Explosives','Water and soap','Their bare hands'],'They worked it free piece by piece.','N'],
['M','Akhenaten is known for',['promoting worship of the Aten, the sun disk','building the Great Pyramid','conquering Rome','writing hieroglyphic dictionaries'],'He broke with the old gods.','N'],
['M','Akhenaten moved Egypt’s capital to',['Amarna','Thebes','Cairo','Giza'],'He built a new city for the Aten.','N'],
['M','Tutankhamun’s original name was',['Tutankhaten','Amenhotep','Smenkhkare','Ramesses'],'He changed it when the old god Amun was restored.','N'],
['M','What was the main purpose of the CT scan?',['To learn more about how he lived and died','To find hidden treasure','To make a film','To decide where to bury him'],'Scientists hoped to answer old questions about his death and age.','N'],
['M','CT in “CT scan” stands for',['computed tomography','carbon testing','chemical tracing','central tomb'],'It combines many X-ray images.','N'],
['M','Which treasure was found with Tut?',['A solid gold coffin','A printing press','Roman coins','Iron weapons from the Middle Ages'],'The tomb was full of dazzling objects.','N'],
['M','“King Tut’s curse” refers to',['the belief that disturbing the tomb brings misfortune','a law passed by Tut','a disease he died of','a song about him'],'It is a popular legend, not a scientific fact.','N'],
['M','Assertion (A): Carter’s team damaged Tut’s mummy.<br>Reason (R): Carter wanted to sell the mummy’s gold.','AR3','A is true. R is false: the team cut the mummy because the hardened resins had cemented it to the coffin.','B'],
['H','Who was Smenkhkare?',['A mysterious ruler who appeared between Akhenaten and Tut','Carter’s assistant','The scanner’s inventor','Tut’s pet'],'Little is known about this ruler.','N'],
['H','Who was possibly Tutankhamun’s grandfather?',['Amenhotep III','Ramesses II','Cleopatra','Howard Carter'],'The chapter describes Amenhotep III as possibly his grandfather.','N'],
['H','Why was the CT scanner placed in a trailer near the tomb?',['So the mummy could be scanned without being moved far','Because the tomb had electricity problems only','For the tourists to see','To hide it from the press'],'The mummy was carried a short distance to the scanner.','N'],
['H','What does “the saga continues” in the title suggest?',['The story and research about Tut are still going on','Tut is still alive','The tomb is still unopened','A film sequel is planned'],'Science keeps adding to the story.','N'],
['H','What is the tone of the chapter?',['Informative and factual','Humorous','Angry','Romantic'],'It reports facts and scientific methods.','N'],
['H','Where was the mummy kept after the scan?',['Back in its tomb','In a London museum','In a bank vault','On a ship'],'It was returned to rest in its tomb.','N'],
['H','The CT scan was historically important because',['it was the first time a mummy like Tut’s was examined this way','it found Tut alive','it proved the curse true','it discovered a new pyramid'],'It was a landmark use of modern technology in archaeology.','N'],
['H','Why were objects buried with Tut?',['To accompany and serve the king in the afterlife','To hide them from thieves','To pay the workers','By mistake'],'Egyptians believed in life after death.','N'],
['H','Assertion (A): Akhenaten is sometimes called a strange or “wacky” king.<br>Reason (R): He promoted worship of one god, the Aten, and broke with the old gods.','AR1','Both are true, and R explains why he is seen as unusual.','B'],
['H','Assertion (A): The CT scan finally answered every question about Tut’s death.<br>Reason (R): The CT scan produced about 1,700 images of the mummy.','AR4','A is false: questions remained, which is why the saga continues. R is true.','B']
]},
{id:'e4',n:4,code:'HB 4',ref:'Hornbill, Chapter 4',libLabel:'4. The Ailing Planet',name:'The Ailing Planet: the Green Movement’s Role',unit:'prose',pdf:'kehb104',
lesson:{intro:'Nani Palkhivala argues that the Earth is sick because humans have overused it, and that the Green Movement has changed our view of the world: we are not owners of the Earth but trustees who must leave it in good health for our children.',
ideas:['The Green Movement brought a shift from a narrow, human-centred view to a holistic and ecological view of the world, a change the author compares to the Copernican revolution.','Sustainable development (Brundtland Commission): development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.','In a zoo in Lusaka, Zambia, a cage labelled “the world’s most dangerous animal” holds a mirror: the most dangerous animal is the human being.','Four principal biological systems support life: fisheries, forests, grasslands and croplands. When human demand exceeds their sustainable yield, they begin to decline.','Forests are being cut faster than they grow, often for fuelwood in poor countries.','Population growth makes every problem worse; the author argues that development is the best contraceptive.','Key idea: we have not inherited the Earth from our forefathers; we have borrowed it from our children.'],
formulas:['Author: Nani Palkhivala · Genre: essay / argumentative article','Sustainable development: needs of the present without harming the needs of the future','Four systems: fisheries, forests, grasslands, croplands','Lusaka zoo mirror: humans are the most dangerous animal','Theme: environmental responsibility; humans as partners, not masters'],
example:'<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>What is the significance of the mirror in the Lusaka zoo?</em> The cage is labelled “the world’s most dangerous animal”, but it holds only a mirror. Visitors see themselves, which shows that humans, by destroying nature, are the most dangerous species on Earth.</p>',
trap:'The four biological systems are fisheries, forests, grasslands and croplands. Deserts, oceans as a whole, or mountains are not in the list.',
shortcut:'Every answer from this chapter can link back to one line: we are trustees of the Earth for future generations.',
exam:'The meaning of sustainable development, the Lusaka zoo mirror, the four biological systems, population and development, and the role of the Green Movement.'},
q:[
['E','Who wrote “The Ailing Planet: the Green Movement’s Role”?',['Nani Palkhivala','Nick Middleton','Khushwant Singh','Jayant Narlikar'],'A noted jurist and economist.','N'],
['E','The word “ailing” in the title means',['sick','ancient','shining','growing'],'The planet is unwell because of human activity.','N'],
['E','In the Lusaka zoo, the cage labelled “the world’s most dangerous animal” contains',['a mirror','a lion','a snake','nothing at all'],'Visitors see themselves.','N'],
['E','Lusaka is in',['Zambia','Kenya','India','Brazil'],'The zoo is in Zambia’s capital.','N'],
['E','Which of these is NOT one of the four principal biological systems?',['Deserts','Fisheries','Forests','Croplands'],'The four are fisheries, forests, grasslands and croplands.','N'],
['E','Sustainable development was defined by the',['Brundtland Commission','United Nations Security Council','World Bank alone','Indian Parliament'],'Named after Gro Harlem Brundtland of Norway.','N'],
['E','The Green Movement encourages a view of the world that is',['holistic and ecological','purely economic','human-centred only','industrial'],'It sees humans as part of nature.','N'],
['E','According to the author, the most dangerous animal is',['the human being','the tiger','the shark','the mosquito'],'That is the point of the mirror.','N'],
['E','“We have not inherited this earth from our forefathers; we have borrowed it from our children” means we must',['protect the Earth for future generations','sell the Earth’s resources','ignore the environment','return to old ways completely'],'We are trustees, not owners.','N'],
['E','Which resource is being cut faster than it can grow back?',['Forests','Sunlight','Wind','Tides'],'Deforestation is a major concern in the chapter.','N'],
['M','Sustainable development means development that',['meets present needs without harming the ability of future generations to meet theirs','uses all resources as fast as possible','stops all industry','benefits only rich countries'],'This is the Brundtland definition.','N'],
['M','The author compares the change brought by the Green Movement to the',['Copernican revolution','Industrial Revolution','French Revolution','Green Revolution in farming'],'It was a basic change in how we see our place in the world.','N'],
['M','What happens when human demand on a biological system exceeds its sustainable yield?',['The system begins to decline','The system grows faster','Nothing changes','The system turns into a desert at once'],'Overuse destroys the productive base.','N'],
['M','Why are forests cut in many poor countries, according to the chapter?',['For fuelwood','For building airports only','For tourism','For mining gold'],'Poor families depend on fuelwood.','N'],
['M','“Development is the best contraceptive” means',['economic development naturally reduces population growth','contraceptives should be banned','development increases population','poor countries should not develop'],'Better living standards lead to smaller families.','N'],
['M','The tone of the chapter is',['serious and persuasive','light and humorous','sad and hopeless','angry and insulting'],'The author argues for urgent action.','N'],
['M','“Holistic” in the chapter means',['looking at the whole system, not just parts','religious','about holes in the ozone layer only','profitable'],'The world is seen as one connected system.','N'],
['M','The main purpose of the chapter is to',['make readers aware of the environmental crisis and our responsibility','describe a holiday in Zambia','praise industrial growth','explain how zoos work'],'It is an argument for environmental responsibility.','N'],
['M','Population growth is a problem in the chapter because it',['increases pressure on limited natural resources','makes the Earth bigger','improves forests','reduces demand for food'],'More people means more demand on the four systems.','N'],
['M','Assertion (A): The Green Movement changed how people see their relationship with nature.<br>Reason (R): The Green Movement holds that humans are the masters of nature.','AR3','A is true. R is false: the movement sees humans as partners of nature, not its masters.','B'],
['H','Why does the author call the shift in thinking a “Copernican revolution”?',['Like Copernicus showing the Earth is not the centre, it shows humans are not the centre of everything','Because Copernicus started the Green Movement','Because it happened in Poland','Because it is about astronomy'],'It is a change of perspective from human-centred to holistic.','N'],
['H','Which pair are both among the four principal biological systems?',['Grasslands and fisheries','Deserts and glaciers','Oceans and mountains','Cities and farms'],'Fisheries, forests, grasslands and croplands.','N'],
['H','The mirror in the Lusaka zoo is an example of',['irony used to make a serious point','a scientific experiment','a zoo decoration','a children’s game'],'The surprise makes readers reflect on human behaviour.','N'],
['H','In the chapter, human beings are described as',['trustees of the Earth for future generations','owners who may use the Earth as they wish','guests with no responsibility','the only species that matters'],'This is the central moral of the essay.','N'],
['H','Which statement would the author most agree with?',['Environmental protection and development must go together','Industry must be stopped completely','Only governments can protect nature','Population has no link with the environment'],'Sustainable development combines both.','N'],
['H','The word “sustainable” suggests something that',['can be kept going over a long time','is very cheap','happens only once','is very fast'],'Use must not exhaust the resource.','N'],
['H','Why is the loss of the four biological systems serious?',['They supply food and raw materials for human life and industry','They are only for decoration','They affect only animals','They can be replaced easily'],'They are the base of the economy and of survival.','N'],
['H','Which device does the author mainly use to persuade?',['Facts, examples and memorable quotations','Long stories about his family','Poetry and rhyme','Jokes'],'Statistics, the Lusaka example and quotations support his case.','N'],
['H','Assertion (A): According to the author, the Earth’s problems can be ignored for another century.<br>Reason (R): The four biological systems are under pressure from rising human demand.','AR4','A is false: the author calls for urgent action. R is true.','B'],
['H','Assertion (A): The author supports sustainable development.<br>Reason (R): Lusaka is the capital of Zambia.','AR2','Both are true, but the location of Lusaka has nothing to do with why he supports sustainable development.','B']
]},
{id:'e5',n:5,code:'HB 5',ref:'Hornbill, Chapter 5',libLabel:'5. The Adventure',name:'The Adventure',unit:'prose',pdf:'kehb105',
lesson:{intro:'Jayant Narlikar’s science-fiction story takes historian Professor Gaitonde into an alternative India where the Marathas won the Third Battle of Panipat. His friend Rajendra Deshpande, a scientist, explains the experience using catastrophe theory and quantum physics.',
ideas:['Professor Gaitonde, a historian, is travelling when his vehicle collides with a truck; he finds himself in a Bombay where the British never ruled India.','In this world, the Marathas won the Third Battle of Panipat (1761) because the young Vishwasrao narrowly escaped a bullet. The East India Company stayed a trading company, and India grew strong under the Peshwas.','Gaitonde reads the alternative history in a library and notices the differences from the history he knew.','At a meeting at Azad Maidan he sits in the empty presiding chair; the crowd objects, he is thrown out, and he finds himself back in his own world.','Rajendra explains with catastrophe theory (small changes can cause sudden big effects) and the quantum idea of many possible worlds, connected through a “lack of interaction”.','Gaitonde’s mind, full of thoughts about Panipat at the moment of the accident, may have made the transition to the other world.'],
formulas:['Author: Jayant Narlikar · Genre: science fiction / fantasy','Key event: Third Battle of Panipat, 1761 — Vishwasrao survives, Marathas win','Characters: Professor Gaitonde (historian), Rajendra Deshpande (scientist)','Ideas: catastrophe theory; quantum theory; many worlds; “lack of interaction”','Theme: history depends on small events; reality may have many versions'],
example:'<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>How did Rajendra explain Gaitonde’s experience?</em> He said that, by catastrophe theory, a small change at a critical moment can cause a large change, and quantum theory allows many worlds to exist. Gaitonde, thinking of Panipat during the accident, may have crossed into a world where the Marathas won.</p>',
trap:'The decisive “small change” in the other world was Vishwasrao escaping the bullet at Panipat. It was not a different king or a different year.',
shortcut:'Pair the two characters with their subjects: Gaitonde = history (what happened), Rajendra = science (how it could happen).',
exam:'Differences between the two worlds, catastrophe theory, the role of Panipat, how Gaitonde returned, and the blend of history and science.'},
q:[
['E','Who wrote “The Adventure”?',['Jayant Narlikar','Nick Middleton','Gordon Cook','Marga Minco'],'A famous Indian astrophysicist and writer.','N'],
['E','Professor Gaitonde was a',['historian','physician','pilot','businessman'],'He knew Maratha history in detail.','N'],
['E','Rajendra Deshpande was a',['scientist','historian','politician','shopkeeper'],'He explains Gaitonde’s experience scientifically.','N'],
['E','Which battle’s different result changed history in the story?',['The Third Battle of Panipat','The Battle of Plassey','The Battle of Waterloo','The Battle of Haldighati'],'It was fought in 1761.','N'],
['E','In the alternative world, who won the Battle of Panipat?',['The Marathas','The Afghans','The British','The Mughals'],'This changed the course of Indian history.','N'],
['E','In which city does Gaitonde find himself in the alternative world?',['Bombay','Delhi','Calcutta','Madras'],'He explores Bombay and its library.','N'],
['E','“The Adventure” belongs to the genre of',['science fiction','biography','travelogue','history textbook'],'It mixes science, history and imagination.','N'],
['E','In which year was the Third Battle of Panipat fought?',['1761','1857','1526','1947'],'January 1761.','N'],
['E','In the other world, the East India Company',['remained only a trading company','ruled all of India','never existed','became a navy'],'The British never gained political power.','N'],
['E','What theory does Rajendra mention to explain how small changes cause big effects?',['Catastrophe theory','The theory of evolution','The big bang theory','Germ theory'],'Small changes at critical points can cause sudden large effects.','N'],
['M','Why did the Marathas win in the alternative world?',['Vishwasrao narrowly escaped a bullet','The British helped them','The Afghans did not arrive','It rained heavily'],'His survival kept the Maratha army together.','N'],
['M','What accident happened just before Gaitonde’s adventure?',['A collision with a truck','A train derailment','A fall from a horse','A boat capsizing'],'He lost consciousness and woke in the other world.','N'],
['M','Where did Gaitonde read the alternative history?',['In a library in Bombay','In his own diary','On a television news channel','In a school textbook he owned'],'He studied the books carefully to see what had changed.','N'],
['M','What did Gaitonde do at the meeting at Azad Maidan?',['He sat in the empty presiding chair','He gave a speech on science','He sold books','He sang a song'],'The crowd objected and he was thrown out.','N'],
['M','What happened after Gaitonde was thrown out of the meeting?',['He found himself back in his own world','He became a Peshwa','He was arrested and jailed for years','He went to England'],'The event triggered his return.','N'],
['M','In the quantum explanation, the different worlds exist side by side because of',['a lack of interaction between them','a magic portal','a time machine','a dream only'],'Rajendra uses this idea to explain the two realities.','N'],
['M','Why was Gaitonde especially likely to make the transition, according to Rajendra?',['His mind was full of thoughts about Panipat at the time of the accident','He had a special machine','He was very old','He wanted to be famous'],'His thoughts were focused on that historical turning point.','N'],
['M','The story suggests that history',['can depend on small, chance events','is fixed and could never have been different','is not worth studying','is only about kings'],'One bullet changed the outcome.','N'],
['M','Gaitonde’s reaction to the alternative world was one of',['curiosity and amazement','boredom','anger','fear only'],'As a historian, he was fascinated by the differences.','N'],
['M','Assertion (A): In the alternative world, the British never ruled India.<br>Reason (R): In the alternative world, the British won the Third Battle of Panipat.','AR3','A is true. R is false: the British did not fight at Panipat; in that world the Marathas won.','B'],
['H','Which pairing of character and subject is correct?',['Gaitonde – history; Rajendra – science','Gaitonde – science; Rajendra – history','Both – history','Both – science'],'The story uses both perspectives.','N'],
['H','What does the story show about the relationship between science and history?',['Science can offer explanations for alternative histories','They have nothing in common','History disproves science','Science replaces history'],'Narlikar blends the two.','N'],
['H','Catastrophe theory in the story is used to explain',['how a small change in a system can cause a sudden, large change','why accidents are always fatal','why the Earth will end','how weather is predicted'],'Vishwasrao’s escape is that small change.','N'],
['H','What is meant by “many worlds” in Rajendra’s explanation?',['Several versions of reality may exist side by side','There are many planets with life','People travel to space','There are many history books'],'An idea from quantum theory.','N'],
['H','What is the significance of Vishwasrao’s escape?',['It was the small event that changed the course of Indian history','It was a minor detail','It made the Afghans win','It led to British rule'],'It is the turning point between the two worlds.','N'],
['H','Which statement best describes the ending?',['Gaitonde returns, and Rajendra’s scientific explanation leaves the reader thinking','Gaitonde stays in the other world forever','The story ends in a war','Rajendra proves it was a dream'],'It ends with an explanation but keeps the mystery.','N'],
['H','Why did the crowd object when Gaitonde sat in the presiding chair?',['It was reserved for the chosen president of the meeting','It was broken','He was too young','He was speaking English'],'In that world, the chair was left empty as a symbol; his action broke the custom.','X'],
['H','Narlikar’s main aim in the story is to',['make readers think about how history and science connect','teach the dates of every battle','describe life in a village','criticise the Marathas'],'It uses fiction to explore real scientific ideas.','N'],
['H','Assertion (A): Gaitonde’s experience is presented as a simple dream with no explanation.<br>Reason (R): Rajendra explains it using catastrophe theory and quantum theory.','AR4','A is false: the story offers a scientific explanation. R is true.','B'],
['H','Assertion (A): Gaitonde noticed differences in Bombay in the alternative world.<br>Reason (R): Gaitonde was a historian.','AR2','Both are true, but being a historian does not explain why Bombay was different; the different outcome at Panipat does.','B']
]},
{id:'e6',n:6,code:'HB 6',ref:'Hornbill, Chapter 6',libLabel:'6. Silk Road',name:'Silk Road',unit:'prose',pdf:'kehb106',
lesson:{intro:'Nick Middleton’s travelogue describes his journey across the high, harsh Tibetan plateau to Mount Kailash, a sacred mountain, to perform the kora (the pilgrim’s walk around it). It is full of vivid landscapes, local people and the difficulties of high altitude.',
ideas:['The narrator travels by car with his driver Tsetan across the Tibetan plateau towards Mount Kailash.','He sees nomads’ tents, herds of yaks and sheep, and fierce Tibetan mastiffs; he also learns that Tibetan antelopes are hunted for their fine wool.','The high altitude and the cold make him ill; at Darchen he sees a doctor, who diagnoses a cold rather than serious altitude sickness.','Hor, on the shore of Lake Mansarovar, strikes him as dirty and dismal, a contrast to the lake’s sacred beauty.','At Darchen he meets Norbu, a Tibetan scholar, who plans to make the kora too.','Mount Kailash is sacred to Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and Bon followers; the kora is the circuit pilgrims walk around it.'],
formulas:['Author: Nick Middleton · Genre: travelogue','Destination: Mount Kailash · Pilgrimage: the kora (circuit around the mountain)','Places: Lake Mansarovar, Hor, Darchen · People: Tsetan (driver), Norbu (scholar)','Challenges: altitude, cold, rough terrain, illness','Themes: the hardship and reward of travel, culture and faith, landscape'],
example:'<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Why was the narrator disappointed by Hor?</em> He expected a town on the shore of sacred Lake Mansarovar to be beautiful, but Hor was a grim, dirty place with no greenery. The contrast between the lake’s holy reputation and the dreary town disappointed him.</p>',
trap:'Tsetan is the driver; Norbu is the Tibetan scholar met at Darchen. Do not swap them.',
shortcut:'Trace the journey in order: plateau and nomads → Hor at Lake Mansarovar → illness at Darchen → meeting Norbu → setting out for the kora.',
exam:'Description of the landscape, the role of Tsetan and Norbu, the narrator’s illness at Darchen, the contrast at Hor, and the meaning of the kora.'},
q:[
['E','Who wrote “Silk Road”?',['Nick Middleton','Nani Palkhivala','A.R. Williams','Jayant Narlikar'],'A travel writer and geographer.','N'],
['E','What was the narrator’s destination?',['Mount Kailash','Mount Everest','Delhi','Beijing'],'A sacred mountain in Tibet.','N'],
['E','What is the kora?',['The pilgrim’s circuit around Mount Kailash','A Tibetan dish','A kind of tent','A mountain pass'],'Pilgrims walk around the sacred mountain.','N'],
['E','Who was the narrator’s driver?',['Tsetan','Norbu','Daniel','Rajendra'],'Tsetan drove skilfully across the plateau.','N'],
['E','“Silk Road” belongs to which genre?',['Travelogue','Science fiction','Drama','Poetry'],'It describes a real journey.','N'],
['E','The journey takes place in',['Tibet','Kerala','the Sahara','Siberia'],'Across the high Tibetan plateau.','N'],
['E','Which animals guard the nomads’ tents in the chapter?',['Mastiffs (large dogs)','Lions','Elephants','Camels'],'Fierce Tibetan mastiffs.','N'],
['E','Which lake is near Hor?',['Lake Mansarovar','Dal Lake','Lake Victoria','Chilika Lake'],'A sacred lake.','N'],
['E','What made the narrator ill on the journey?',['The high altitude and cold','Food poisoning in a city','A snake bite','A car accident'],'Thin air and cold are hard on travellers.','N'],
['E','Who is Norbu?',['A Tibetan scholar the narrator meets at Darchen','The narrator’s driver','A doctor in Hor','A mountain guide from Nepal'],'He plans to make the kora too.','N'],
['M','How did the narrator find Hor?',['Grim, dirty and dismal','Beautiful and green','Crowded with tourists','Full of temples'],'It contrasted with the sacred lake.','N'],
['M','Where did the narrator see a doctor?',['At Darchen','In Lhasa','In Hor','In Kathmandu'],'He went to the medical centre at Darchen.','N'],
['M','What did the doctor say was wrong with the narrator?',['He had a cold rather than serious altitude sickness','He had a broken leg','He had malaria','Nothing at all'],'He was given medicine and advice.','N'],
['M','Why is Mount Kailash important?',['It is sacred to several religions','It is the tallest mountain in the world','It has a large city','It is a ski resort'],'Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and Bon followers revere it.','N'],
['M','Why are Tibetan antelopes hunted, according to the chapter?',['For their fine wool','For their milk','For racing','For their horns used as tools'],'Their underfur is made into valuable shawls.','N'],
['M','Which word best describes the Tibetan landscape in the chapter?',['Vast, high and harsh','Tropical and humid','Flat and crowded','Green and marshy'],'A cold, high plateau.','N'],
['M','What is Tsetan’s role in the journey?',['He is a skilful driver who guides the narrator across difficult terrain','He is a cook','He is a monk','He is a tourist'],'He finds routes across rough ground and snow.','N'],
['M','What does the narrator learn from meeting Norbu?',['That he has a companion for the kora','That the trip is cancelled','That Kailash is closed','That Hor is a beautiful town'],'Norbu, too, plans to go around the mountain.','N'],
['M','The chapter mainly presents the journey through',['vivid description of places, people and difficulties','dialogue only','statistics','poems'],'It is a descriptive travel account.','N'],
['M','Assertion (A): The narrator was disappointed by Hor.<br>Reason (R): Hor was a dirty, dismal place despite being on the shore of a sacred lake.','AR1','Both are true, and R explains A.','B'],
['H','What contrast does the narrator draw at Hor?',['Between the holy reputation of Lake Mansarovar and the dreary town','Between rich and poor travellers','Between old and new cars','Between summer and winter'],'Beauty and holiness on one side, dirt and gloom on the other.','N'],
['H','Why is the journey to Kailash described as difficult?',['Because of the altitude, cold, rough terrain and illness','Because of heavy traffic','Because of a war','Because of a lack of maps only'],'The physical challenges are a major part of the story.','N'],
['H','What does the kora mean to pilgrims?',['A spiritual act of devotion','A sports race','A shopping trip','A government survey'],'Walking around the mountain is an act of faith.','N'],
['H','Which statement best describes the narrator’s attitude?',['Curious and determined despite hardships','Bored and impatient','Afraid and ready to turn back at once','Uninterested in local culture'],'He keeps going and observes closely.','N'],
['H','The detail about mastiffs and nomads’ tents helps to',['show the way of life on the Tibetan plateau','describe city life','explain the narrator’s illness','describe Hor’s hotels'],'It builds a picture of local culture.','N'],
['H','The title “Silk Road” refers to',['the ancient trade route region the narrator’s travels relate to','a road made of silk','a shop selling silk','a street in Hor'],'The Silk Road was an old trade network across Asia.','X'],
['H','Which pair is matched correctly?',['Tsetan – driver; Norbu – scholar','Tsetan – scholar; Norbu – driver','Tsetan – doctor; Norbu – monk','Tsetan – tourist; Norbu – guide'],'Tsetan drives; Norbu is met at Darchen.','N'],
['H','What is the main theme of the chapter?',['Travel as a test of endurance that brings rich experiences','The dangers of science','Family conflict','City pollution'],'Hardship and reward go together on the journey.','N'],
['H','Assertion (A): Mount Kailash is sacred only to Buddhists.<br>Reason (R): Pilgrims walk the kora around Mount Kailash.','AR4','A is false: it is sacred to Hindus, Jains and Bon followers too. R is true.','B'],
['H','Assertion (A): The narrator fell ill on the journey.<br>Reason (R): Tsetan was an expert driver.','AR2','Both are true, but Tsetan’s driving does not explain the illness; altitude and cold do.','B']
]}
);
